{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789648101,"nonce":"O06S3TN0d9DcdWE0d/QKlzXJsEM=","message":"Rayzor and Ash comparison for TieredRuntime interpreter-first requirement: Ash's normal call_function path invokes tiered_on_invoke for every bytecode call, dispatches a compiled entry only when beadie returns one, and otherwise executes the bytecode function. Its loop backedge also ticks beadie and transfers only through an OSR entry; compiled-only behavior is a separate explicit mode. Rayzor stores the interpreted tier at module load and chooses interpreter/native in execute_function, though it has SIMD and JIT-only exceptions. In Zyntax, TieredBackend::compile_module_lazily precompiles Cranelift and eager_install()s every bead; TieredRuntime::call_raw then obtains that native pointer and calls it. Zyntax's compiled-to-compiled calls use reload cells initialized to native pointers, so deleting eager_install alone neither provides interpreter dispatch nor prevents cold callees being entered natively. HirInterpreter exists but uses its own allocation/global storage, does not execute effects/fibers, and its JIT bridge has restricted ABI shapes. A correct port needs one TieredRuntime-owned interpreter dispatch path, beadie uncompiled initial state, interpreter-aware call cells/stubs for native-to-cold calls, shared allocation/global semantics, interpreter loop OSR, and tests proving first entry and callee entry are interpreted before promotion. No implementation landed yet.","files":null}]}